---
question: What is Separation Logic?
---

[Separation logic](https://cacm.acm.org/research/separation-logic/) (SL) is a general-purpose logic for reasoning about independence, the principle that underlies all design principles. Rather than being tightly tied to any specific language, separation logic abstracts the actual state and focuses on the way the state can be divided and reasoned about independently. This abstraction has enabled separation logic to be applied in a wide range of reasoning domains including: a range of work on [weak memory](), [distributed systems](), and even [probability theory]().
Many of these applications were beyond the original motivation of separation logic, but, just like modern scientific theories, separation logic has repeatedly demonstrated its firm grasp on the underlying problem.

[//]: shared, mutable data. The core concept in separation logic is the idea of logical independence and the power and generality of the way that separation logic has been demonstrated over and over to elegantly capture many problems that it was not originally developed to specifically work on. Some key illuminating examples of the power and flexibility of separation logic include: [fine-grained concurrent programs](), [weak memory](), [independence in probability theory]().

